  [1mThe fork:[0m you asked for [38;5;153muserId[39m as a [3mparameter[0m, but the submit handler at [38;5;153mapp.js:19-23[39m has no user ID to pass — it only
  has the username and password typed into the form. Login is the thing that establishes identity; an ID is normally
  something it [3mreturns[0m, not something it receives. So "track who logged in" could mean two different changes, and they
  differ in who has to change:

  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m as a new parameter[0m (literally what you asked) — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m. Buys you exactly the
    signature you described. Costs: every caller must now produce a user ID [3mbefore[0m authenticating, and today's only
    caller can't; I'd have to invent a source (a form field, a generated client-side ID, a lookup). It also changes an
    interface — anything outside this file calling [38;5;153mlogin()[39m breaks. Cheap to change later only if callers stay at one.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m on the return value[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m and returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m. Buys you the
    tracking without touching any caller's arguments. Costs: the ID has to come from the auth response, which is a stub
    here ([38;5;153mapp.js:6[39m), so it'd be a placeholder until the real POST exists. Additive, so nothing breaks.
  - [1mA separate tracking call[0m — leave [38;5;153mlogin()[39m alone, log the event alongside it in the handler. Buys separation of auth
    from telemetry. Costs: a second thing to keep in sync; probably overkill at this size.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the return value. "Track who logged in" is satisfied by login [3mreporting[0m the identity, and it's the
  only option where the existing caller keeps working unchanged. If you specifically need the parameter — say, a
